 client validated against and the target actually written cannot drift
 apart (§1.1 O1). Off the cyclic tree — pTarget must never reach a client.]]></Comment><BitSize>100608</BitSize><BitOffs>164352</BitOffs><Properties><Property><Name>OPC.UA.DA</Name><Value>0</Value></Property></Properties></SubItem><SubItem><Name>_nConfigWrite</Name><Type>INT</Type><BitSize>16</BitSize><BitOffs>264960</BitOffs><Properties><Property><Name>OPC.UA.DA</Name><Value>0</Value></Property></Properties></SubItem><SubItem><Name>_pConfigWrite</Name><Type Namespace="Fraktal_Core" PointerTo="1">ST_ConfigWriteDef</Type><BitSize>64</BitSize><BitOffs>265024</BitOffs><Properties><Property><Name>OPC.UA.DA</Name><Value>0</Value></Property></Properties></SubItem><SubItem><Name>_configCapacity</Name><Type>INT</Type><BitSize>16</BitSize><BitOffs>265088</BitOffs><Default><Value>12</Value></Default></SubItem><SubItem><Name>_pModelRegion</Name><Type GUID="{18071995-0000-0000-0000-000000000018}">PVOID</Type><Comment><![CDATA[ §3.8a - this module's own recipe record, and the record a request is
 viewing instead of it (0 = the live one). See _M_SetModelRegion.]]></Comment><BitSize>64</BitSize><BitOffs>265152</BitOffs><Properties><Property><Name>OPC.UA.DA</Name><Value>0</Value></Property></Properties></SubItem><SubItem><Name>_modelRegionSize</Name><Type>UDINT</Type><BitSize>32</BitSize><BitOffs>265216</BitOffs></SubItem><SubItem><Name>_pModelView</Name><Type GUID="{18071995-0000-0000-0000-000000000018}">PVOID</Type><BitSize>64</BitSize><BitOffs>265280</BitOffs><Properties><Property><Name>OPC.UA.DA</Name><Value>0</Value></Property></Properties></SubItem><SubItem><Name>_restoreLost</Name><Type>BOOL</Type><Comment><![CDATA[ §3.8b — one restore-loss slot. ONE, not a list: a module with two retained
 structures that both lost their schema lost them for the same reason (its
 own layout changed), and one annunciation naming the module is exactly
 what the operator needs. The slot is cleared as the root takes it, so the
 event is raised exactly once per boot.]]></Comment><BitSize>8</BitSize><BitOffs>265344</BitOffs><Properties><Property><Name>OPC.UA.DA</Name><Value>0</Value></Property></Properties></SubItem><SubItem><Name>_restoreExpected</Name><Type>UINT</Type><BitSize>16</BitSize><BitOffs>265360</BitOffs><Properties><Property><Name>OPC.UA.DA</Name><Value>0</Value></Property></Properties></SubItem><SubItem><Name>_restoreFound</Name><Type>UINT</Type><BitSize>16</BitSize><BitOffs>265376</BitOffs><Properties><Property><Name>OPC.UA.DA</Name><Value>0</Value></Property></Properties></SubItem><Method><Name>RequestHeldCommand</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ §7.6.1 - the entry point for a HELD manual command, called EVERY SCAN while
 the operator holds the control. Silence is a stop: a module that implements
 this shall stop the action when the calls stop, whether the operator let go or
 the transport died (O10 - a lost connection queues nothing).

 Enable carries the §9 manual-enable fact for actions that need one (motion
 with the guards open). It is STATUS, never authority: the module still applies
 its own interlocks, and the safety system remains what actually permits the
 movement. Modules with no held commands inherit this refusal unchanged.]]></Comment><ReturnType>BOOL</ReturnType><ReturnBitSize>8</ReturnBitSize><Parameter><Name>Value</Name><Type>DINT</Type><BitSize>32</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>Enable</Name><Type Namespace="Fraktal_Core">ST_ManualEnableStatus</Type><BitSize>664</BitSize></Parameter></Method><Method><Name>M_RegisterCapture</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ §3.8c - offer a capture for an ALREADY-REGISTERED editable value. Called at
 Setup, after the M_RegisterConfigWrite that declares the field.

 Refused when the key is not registered, which is §3.8c(a) enforced where it
 cannot be bypassed: capture changes how the number arrives, never which
 numbers may change. pSource shall point at a value the module already
 publishes (§3.8c(b)) - the operator has to be able to see the number that is
 about to be stored, on the same screen, before storing it.]]></Comment><ReturnType>BOOL</ReturnType><ReturnBitSize>8</ReturnBitSize><Parameter><Name>WriteKey</Name><Type>STRING(160)</Type><BitSize>1288</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>pSource</Name><Type GUID="{18071995-0000-0000-0000-000000000018}">PVOID</Type><BitSize>64</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>SourceStorage</Name><Type Namespace="Fraktal_Core">E_ConfigStorage</Type><BitSize>32</BitSize></Parameter><Local><Name>i</Name><Type>INT</Type><BitSize>16</BitSize></Local></Method><Method><Name>_M_UseConfigRegistry</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ Optional owner storage selected before any registration; base modules keep twelve slots.]]></Comment><ReturnType>BOOL</ReturnType><ReturnBitSize>8</ReturnBitSize><Parameter><Name>pRecords</Name><Type Namespace="Fraktal_Core" PointerTo="1">ST_ConfigWriteDef</Type><BitSize>64</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>Capacity</Name><Type>INT</Type><BitSize>16</BitSize></Parameter></Method><Method><Name>M_StageConfigWrite</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ §3.8b I_ConfigSource — would this write be accepted? Byte-for-byte the
 lookup and gating of M_ApplyConfigWrite, then the shared validator with
 Commit := FALSE. Nothing in this module changes, including through the
 type-specific invariant hook, which §3.10.2 already requires to be free of
 side effects because it runs on a candidate that may still be rejected.]]></Comment><ReturnType>BOOL</ReturnType><ReturnBitSize>8</ReturnBitSize><Parameter><Name>Scope</Name><Type>STRING(255)</Type><BitSize>2048</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>WriteKey</Name><Type>STRING(160)</Type><BitSize>1288</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>Revision</Name><Type>UDINT</Type><BitSize>32</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>TextValue</Name><Type>STRING(255)</Type><BitSize>2048</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>RootReady</Name><Type>BOOL</Type><BitSize>8</BitSize></Parameter><Local><Name>i</Name><Type>INT</Type><BitSize>16</BitSize></Local></Method><Method><Name>M_ApplyCapture</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ §3.8c - apply a capture to THIS module. Resolves the live value and then hands
 it to the ordinary write path, so validation, the type's own invariants, range,
 domain and storage are the SAME code a typed write goes through. A machine-
 supplied number is not more trustworthy than a human-supplied one (§14).]]></Comment><ReturnType>BOOL</ReturnType><ReturnBitSize>8</ReturnBitSize><Parameter><Name>Scope</Name><Type>STRING(255)</Type><BitSize>2048</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>WriteKey</Name><Type>STRING(160)</Type><BitSize>1288</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>Revision</Name><Type>UDINT</Type><BitSize>32</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>RootReady</Name><Type>BOOL</Type><BitSize>8</BitSize></Parameter><Local><Name>i</Name><Type>INT</Type><BitSize>16</BitSize></Local></Method><Method><Name>M_RegisterConfigDuration</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ §3.8b — an editable TIME, carried and edited as whole milliseconds (the HMI
 validates DURATION as a number; TC3 §3.8 explains the enum spelling).]]></Comment><ReturnType>BOOL</ReturnType><ReturnBitSize>8</ReturnBitSize><Parameter><Name>Key</Name><Type>STRING(160)</Type><BitSize>1288</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>Item</Name><Type>STRING(255)</Type><BitSize>2048</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>LabelKey</Name><Type>STRING(160)</Type><BitSize>1288</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>Kind</Name><Type Namespace="Fraktal_Core">E_ConfigKind</Type><BitSize>32</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>pTarget</Name><Type PointerTo="1">TIME</Type><BitSize>64</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>MinimumMs</Name><Type>UDINT</Type><BitSize>32</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>MaximumMs</Name><Type>UDINT</Type><BitSize>32</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>RequiresReady</Name><Type>BOOL</Type><BitSize>8</BitSize></Parameter></Method><Method><Name>CommitRecipe</Name></Method><Method><Name>_M_SweepStateFlags</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ §3.12 — resolve the previous scan's publications, before this scan re-publishes.
 A flag the owner stopped computing stops claiming; nothing has to be
 deregistered when a module stops publishing one (§1.1 O1).]]></Comment><Local><Name>i</Name><Type>INT</Type><BitSize>16</BitSize></Local></Method><Method><Name>_M_PublishCommand</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ §7.6.1 — register one manual command in the catalog (call in the module's init).
 Idempotent by Value: re-publishing a Value updates its label.]]></Comment><Parameter><Name>Value</Name><Type>DINT</Type><BitSize>32</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>Label</Name><Type>STRING(120)</Type><BitSize>968</BitSize></Parameter><Local><Name>i</Name><Type>INT</Type><BitSize>16</BitSize></Local></Method><Method><Name>M_RegisterConfigFlag</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ §3.8b — an editable BOOL. The exact domain is declared so a client renders a
 choice rather than a free-text field, and so 'yes' is rejected rather than
 silently read as FALSE.]]></Comment><ReturnType>BOOL</ReturnType><ReturnBitSize>8</ReturnBitSize><Parameter><Name>Key</Name><Type>STRING(160)</Type><BitSize>1288</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>Item</Name><Type>STRING(255)</Type><BitSize>2048</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>LabelKey</Name><Type>STRING(160)</Type><BitSize>1288</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>Kind</Name><Type Namespace="Fraktal_Core">E_ConfigKind</Type><BitSize>32</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>pTarget</Name><Type PointerTo="1">BOOL</Type><BitSize>64</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>RequiresReady</Name><Type>BOOL</Type><BitSize>8</BitSize></Parameter></Method><Method><Name>_M_ConfigReadOnly</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ §3.8e - TRUE while this module's registered values may not be changed by a
 client: still published, as Writable := FALSE, and refused by the write and
 staging paths. The base is always writable; a line-data MIRROR is not.]]></Comment><ReturnType>BOOL</ReturnType><ReturnBitSize>8</ReturnBitSize></Method><Method><Name>_M_PublishStatus</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ Called after the common Status mirror refresh. Derived tiers may overlay data.]]></Comment></Method><Method><Name>M_ApplyManual</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ §7.6.1 I_ManualTarget - answer for THIS module only. Same shape as
 M_ApplyConfigWrite: the walk asks every module and exactly the one whose
 Scope matches replies, so routing is a property of registration rather than
 of a chain somebody maintains by hand.]]></Comment><ReturnType>BOOL</ReturnType><ReturnBitSize>8</ReturnBitSize><Parameter><Name>Scope</Name><Type>STRING(255)</Type><BitSize>2048</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>Value</Name><Type>DINT</Type><BitSize>32</BitSize></Parameter></Method><Method><Name>_M_ReadCaptureText</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ §3.8c - read the live source as text, so the capture rejoins the ordinary
 write path instead of growing a second typed store. One conversion, one
 validator, one storage routine.]]></Comment><ReturnType>STRING(255)</ReturnType><ReturnBitSize>2048</ReturnBitSize><Parameter><Name>Idx</Name><Type>INT</Type><BitSize>16</BitSize></Parameter><Local><Name>pB</Name><Type PointerTo="1">BOOL</Type><BitSize>64</BitSize></Local><Local><Name>pI</Name><Type PointerTo="1">INT</Type><BitSize>64</BitSize></Local><Local><Name>pD</Name><Type PointerTo="1">DINT</Type><BitSize>64</BitSize></Local><Local><Name>pU</Name><Type PointerTo="1">UDINT</Type><BitSize>64</BitSize></Local><Local><Name>pR</Name><Type PointerTo="1">REAL</Type><BitSize>64</BitSize></Local><Local><Name>pL</Name><Type PointerTo="1">LREAL</Type><BitSize>64</BitSize></Local><Local><Name>pT</Name><Type PointerTo="1">TIME</Type><BitSize>64</BitSize></Local><Local><Name>pS</Name><Type PointerTo="1">STRING(80)</Type><BitSize>64</BitSize></Local></Method><Method><Name>M_ReleaseCommand</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ Core §6.1/§8.3(b) — release a command this module can no longer be driven to
 finish, so the inherited Execute-drop reset clears a latched terminal state on
 the next scan. Deliberately does NOT touch _exec: that transition stays owned by
 Cyclic, preserving the §6.1 reset-provenance rule (IMPLEMENTATION_NOTES §47).
 A live cause therefore re-faults on the next command edge rather than vanishing.]]></Comment><ReturnType>BOOL</ReturnType><ReturnBitSize>8</ReturnBitSize></Method><Method><Name>M_ApplyConfigWrite</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ §3.8b/§3.10.2 I_ConfigSource — apply one registered write to THIS module.
 The root has already checked DATA_WRITE and subtree ownership; what is left is
 everything only the owner knows. Scope selects the module, so the recursive
 walk asks each module in turn and exactly the one that registered the key
 answers TRUE. An unknown key, a stale revision, or a value the module refuses
 all return FALSE and change nothing.]]></Comment><ReturnType>BOOL</ReturnType><ReturnBitSize>8</ReturnBitSize><Parameter><Name>Scope</Name><Type>STRING(255)</Type><BitSize>2048</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>WriteKey</Name><Type>STRING(160)</Type><BitSize>1288</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>Revision</Name><Type>UDINT</Type><BitSize>32</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>TextValue</Name><Type>STRING(255)</Type><BitSize>2048</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>RootReady</Name><Type>BOOL</Type><BitSize>8</BitSize></Parameter><Local><Name>i</Name><Type>INT</Type><BitSize>16</BitSize></Local></Method><Method><Name>M_ConfigAccessOf</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ §3.8d I_ConfigSource - this module's own registry only; composites recurse.]]></Comment><ReturnType>BOOL</ReturnType><ReturnBitSize>8</ReturnBitSize><Parameter><Name>Scope</Name><Type>STRING(255)</Type><BitSize>2048</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>WriteKey</Name><Type>STRING(160)</Type><BitSize>1288</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>Grant</Name><Type Namespace="Fraktal_Core" ReferenceTo="true">ST_ConfigAccess</Type><BitSize>64</BitSize><Properties><Property><Name>ItemType</Name><Value>InOut</Value></Property></Properties></Parameter><Local><Name>i</Name><Type>INT</Type><BitSize>16</BitSize></Local></Method><Method><Name>PrepareRecipe</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ Core §3.8 — types with ParCfg load into staging here.]]></Comment><ReturnType>BOOL</ReturnType><ReturnBitSize>8</ReturnBitSize><Parameter><Name>Model</Name><Type Namespace="Fraktal_Core">ST_ModelId</Type><BitSize>7856</BitSize></Parameter></Method><Method><Name>_M_StoreConfigValue</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ §3.8b/§5.6 — validate a candidate completely, then store it. Every rejection
 happens before the first byte moves, so a refused write leaves the module
 exactly as it was. Range is checked against BOTH the declared bounds and the
 storage type's own limits: a DINT field with no declared maximum still cannot
 be handed 1E30.

 Commit := FALSE runs the IDENTICAL validation and stores nothing. That is the
 staging pass of a §3.8b set load, and it deliberately shares this code rather
 than restating the rules: a validator that can disagree with the writer is
 worse than no validator, because it makes an all-or-nothing load claim an
 atomicity it does not have.]]></Comment><ReturnType>BOOL</ReturnType><ReturnBitSize>8</ReturnBitSize><Parameter><Name>Idx</Name><Type>INT</Type><BitSize>16</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>TextValue</Name><Type>STRING(255)</Type><BitSize>2048</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>Commit</Name><Type>BOOL</Type><BitSize>8</BitSize></Parameter><Local><Name>num</Name><Type>LREAL</Type><BitSize>64</BitSize></Local><Local><Name>pB</Name><Type PointerTo="1">BOOL</Type><BitSize>64</BitSize></Local><Local><Name>pI</Name><Type PointerTo="1">INT</Type><BitSize>64</BitSize></Local><Local><Name>pD</Name><Type PointerTo="1">DINT</Type><BitSize>64</BitSize></Local><Local><Name>pU</Name><Type PointerTo="1">UDINT</Type><BitSize>64</BitSize></Local><Local><Name>pR</Name><Type PointerTo="1">REAL</Type><BitSize>64</BitSize></Local><Local><Name>pL</Name><Type PointerTo="1">LREAL</Type><BitSize>64</BitSize></Local><Local><Name>pT</Name><Type PointerTo="1">TIME</Type><BitSize>64</BitSize></Local><Local><Name>pS</Name><Type PointerTo="1">STRING(80)</Type><BitSize>64</BitSize></Local></Method><Method><Name>OnAbort</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ Core §3.14 hook — optional override: device-safe abort action.
 Override contract (Core §3.14.2): call SUPER^.OnAbort() FIRST and keep its return.]]></Comment><ReturnType>DINT</ReturnType><ReturnBitSize>32</ReturnBitSize></Method><Method><Name>_M_Hold</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ Core §6.1 HELD — suspend progress on a NAMED condition instead of faulting.
 Use it where the condition is expected operator or process behaviour rather than
 a defect: an interlock lost mid-motion, a hold-to-run permissive released. The
 module stays BUSY, so the owner keeps awaiting it and resumes when the condition
 returns; no ERROR, no MANUAL_RESET alarm, nothing to reset. Call it from dispatch
 EVERY scan the condition is unsatisfied — the base clears the hold as soon as a
 scan stops re-asserting it. Withdrawing the outputs stays the caller's job: only
 the device type knows which of its outputs are unsafe to leave energized.]]></Comment><Parameter><Name>Reason</Name><Type Namespace="Fraktal_Core">E_Reason</Type><BitSize>32</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>Text</Name><Type>STRING(255)</Type><BitSize>2048</BitSize></Parameter></Method><Method><Name>_M_Complete</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ Core §6.1 — successful completion (the concrete type publishes OutCmd before calling)]]></Comment></Method><Method><Name>_M_Target</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ Where value Idx lives for THIS request: its registered target, or - while a
 model view is open - the same offset in the viewed model's record.]]></Comment><ReturnType GUID="{18071995-0000-0000-0000-000000000018}">PVOID</ReturnType><ReturnBitSize>64</ReturnBitSize><Parameter><Name>Idx</Name><Type>INT</Type><BitSize>16</BitSize></Parameter></Method><Method><Name>_M_FaultIoN</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ Type-band fault with the implicated schematic tag and terminal location.]]></Comment><Parameter><Name>Code</Name><Type>DINT</Type><BitSize>32</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>Text</Name><Type>STRING(255)</Type><BitSize>2048</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>IoTag</Name><Type>STRING(80)</Type><BitSize>648</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>IoAddress</Name><Type>STRING(80)</Type><BitSize>648</BitSize></Parameter></Method><Method><Name>OnAbortInError</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ Core §3.14 — optional error-state abort/recovery action. ERROR remains latched
 until Execute drops. Overrides call SUPER^.OnAbortInError() first.]]></Comment><ReturnType>DINT</ReturnType><ReturnBitSize>32</ReturnBitSize></Method><Method><Name>M_RestoreStationCfg</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ §3.8b — the restore rule for one retained StationCfg image, written ONCE here
 so no module type carries its own copy of it (§1.1 O1).

 Every StationCfg structure shall carry SchemaVersion : UINT as its FIRST
 member, and this decides what a given value of it means:

   version = SchemaVersion  -> restored. Returns TRUE; the caller keeps the
                               retained values and does nothing.
   version = 0              -> never written. A first boot has lost nothing,
                               so it initializes SILENTLY: returns FALSE with
                               no annunciation, and the caller writes defaults.
   anything else            -> a REAL image was rejected. Returns FALSE and
                               records the loss for the root to annunciate with
                               this module's canonical path.

 The version is stamped forward either way, so the next boot is an ordinary
 restore rather than a repeated annunciation. What is NOT done is reading the
 unrecognized image by field position: that is precisely how a changed layout
 becomes a plausible wrong number instead of an honest default.

 The consequence of a loss belongs to the deployment, not to this module —
 E_ConfigRestorePolicy at the root decides whether the station runs and
 annunciates or refuses START_STOP until an ENGINEER acknowledges. A module
 that faulted itself here would take that decision away from the deployment.]]></Comment><ReturnType>BOOL</ReturnType><ReturnBitSize>8</ReturnBitSize><Parameter><Name>pVersion</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ ADR of the structure's FIRST member]]></Comment><Type PointerTo="1">UINT</Type><BitSize>64</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>SchemaVersion</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ the layout this build understands]]></Comment><Type>UINT</Type><BitSize>16</BitSize></Parameter></Method><Method><Name>AbortRecipe</Name></Method><Method><Name>__getName</Name><ReturnType>STRING(80)</ReturnType><ReturnBitSize>648</ReturnBitSize><Local><Name>Name</Name><Type>STRING(80)</Type><BitSize>648</BitSize></Local><Properties><Property><Name>property</Name></Property></Properties></Method><Method><Name>__getState</Name><ReturnType Namespace="Fraktal_Core">E_ExecState</ReturnType><ReturnBitSize>32</ReturnBitSize><Local><Name>State</Name><Type Namespace="Fraktal_Core">E_ExecState</Type><BitSize>32</BitSize></Local><Properties><Property><Name>property</Name></Property></Properties></Method><Method><Name>ManualCommand</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ §7.6.1 — apply a manual command, routed THROUGH the normal command surface so
 interlocks/first-out/timing still apply (§3.2). The owning Unit is the single
 gate: it calls this ONLY after verifying MANUAL mode (§3.4) + MANUAL access
 (§7.7) and logs the §8.3 event. §7.6 Release<Function> is enforced by the
 module's own _M_Dispatch. Concrete modules read _manualReq/_manualCmd there.]]></Comment><ReturnType>BOOL</ReturnType><ReturnBitSize>8</ReturnBitSize><Parameter><Name>Value</Name><Type>DINT</Type><BitSize>32</BitSize></Parameter><Local><Name>i</Name><Type>INT</Type><BitSize>16</BitSize></Local><Local><Name>supported</Name><Type>BOOL</Type><BitSize>8</BitSize></Local></Method><Method><Name>M_AppendConfig</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ §3.10.2 I_ConfigSource — export the base-owned static facets this module
 deliberately keeps off the cyclic OPC UA tree. Item fragments reproduce the
 server's array naming so the HMI rebuilds the exact browse paths generically.
 A type with additional static data overrides, calls SUPER^ first, appends.]]></Comment><ReturnType>BOOL</ReturnType><ReturnBitSize>8</ReturnBitSize><Parameter><Name>Pager</Name><Type Namespace="Fraktal_Core" ReferenceTo="true">FB_ConfigPager</Type><BitSize>64</BitSize></Parameter><Local><Name>i</Name><Type>INT</Type><BitSize>16</BitSize></Local><Local><Name>item</Name><Type>STRING(255)</Type><BitSize>2048</BitSize></Local></Method><Method><Name>_M_State</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ §3.12 — publish a DERIVED state flag and return it, so the assignment reads
 exactly as it would without the framework:

     OutImm.Homed := _M_State(Idx := 1, Key := 'project.state.atHome',
         Ok := Ram.OutImm.Retracted AND Door.OutImm.Retracted);

 What the call adds over a bare assignment is everything the operator and the
 HMI need and nobody wants to write per flag (§1.1 O1): the flag carries its
 own name, and the moment it last changed (§2.7), and it is published on a
 bounded generic table the HMI renders without knowing the module type (§3.13).
 Same shape as the §6.9(b) named condition wait, deliberately - one idiom for
 "a named boolean the framework publishes" (§1.1 O9).

 CALL IT UNCONDITIONALLY, every scan. A flag that stops being published goes
 Stale and its Value is forced FALSE on the next scan: state that nobody is
 computing is not a claim. That is the point of the mechanism - a latch left
 standing is exactly the failure it exists to prevent.

 Out-of-range Idx is skipped rather than faulted, matching _M_Await (§6.9(b)):
 the value still returns, so a mis-indexed flag loses its publication, never
 the machine logic that reads it.]]></Comment><ReturnType>BOOL</ReturnType><ReturnBitSize>8</ReturnBitSize><Parameter><Name>Idx</Name><Type>INT</Type><BitSize>16</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>Key</Name><Type>STRING(120)</Type><BitSize>968</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>Ok</Name><Type>BOOL</Type><BitSize>8</BitSize></Parameter></Method><Method><Name>_M_AppendCapabilities</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ §3.8b — every value this module declared editable. The value is read live and
 the metadata comes from the same record the write will be validated against,
 so a client can never be shown a capability the PLC would not honour. Scope
 and ItemPrefix exist for the one module published under another's scope: a
 line, which a root presents as its own line data (§3.8e).]]></Comment><Parameter><Name>Pager</Name><Type Namespace="Fraktal_Core" ReferenceTo="true">FB_ConfigPager</Type><BitSize>64</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>Scope</Name><Type>STRING(255)</Type><BitSize>2048</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>ItemPrefix</Name><Type>STRING(16)</Type><BitSize>136</BitSize></Parameter><Local><Name>i</Name><Type>INT</Type><BitSize>16</BitSize></Local></Method><Method><Name>ExecuteCommand</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ Core §3.2 — generic DINT command path; the catalog is the supported set.
 Concrete dispatch consumes the inherited manual request, so typed/generic/HMI
 paths share validation, timing, interlocks, and the PLCopen lifecycle.]]></Comment><ReturnType>BOOL</ReturnType><ReturnBitSize>8</ReturnBitSize><Parameter><Name>Command</Name><Type>DINT</Type><BitSize>32</BitSize></Parameter></Method><Method><Name>M_RegisterConfigWrite</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ §3.8b/§3.10.2 — declare one editable value, at Setup. Registration is the ONLY
 thing that makes a value writable; publication, browsability and DATA_WRITE
 never are. All inputs are explicit for TwinCAT 4024 compatibility.

 A duplicate key is refused and the FIRST registration stands, because nothing
 here can tell which of the two the author meant. The refusal travels back
 through Setup, where a composition root sees it — rather than an operator
 discovering it later at a control that writes the wrong field.]]></Comment><ReturnType>BOOL</ReturnType><ReturnBitSize>8</ReturnBitSize><Parameter><Name>Key</Name><Type>STRING(160)</Type><BitSize>1288</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>Item</Name><Type>STRING(255)</Type><BitSize>2048</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>Revision</Name><Type>UDINT</Type><BitSize>32</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>Kind</Name><Type Namespace="Fraktal_Core">E_ConfigKind</Type><BitSize>32</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>ValueType</Name><Type Namespace="Fraktal_Core">E_ConfigValueType</Type><BitSize>32</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>Storage</Name><Type Namespace="Fraktal_Core">E_ConfigStorage</Type><BitSize>32</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>pTarget</Name><Type GUID="{18071995-0000-0000-0000-000000000018}">PVOID</Type><BitSize>64</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>RequiresReady</Name><Type>BOOL</Type><BitSize>8</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>HasMinimum</Name><Type>BOOL</Type><BitSize>8</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>HasMaximum</Name><Type>BOOL</Type><BitSize>8</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>Minimum</Name><Type>LREAL</Type><BitSize>64</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>Maximum</Name><Type>LREAL</Type><BitSize>64</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>Unit</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ NONE = no unit; the HMI shows none]]></Comment><Type Namespace="Fraktal_Core">E_EngUnit</Type><BitSize>32</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>LabelKey</Name><Type>STRING(160)</Type><BitSize>1288</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>MaxLength</Name><Type>UINT</Type><BitSize>16</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>EnumDomain</Name><Type>STRING(255)</Type><BitSize>2048</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>EnumLabelKey</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ '' = the values are shown as they are]]></Comment><Type>STRING(80)</Type><BitSize>648</BitSize></Parameter><Local><Name>i</Name><Type>INT</Type><BitSize>16</BitSize></Local></Method><Method><Name>_M_Fault</Name><Commen