 appears — so these tests read exactly what the HMI would be given, not an
 internal shortcut into the registry.]]></Comment><ReturnType>BOOL</ReturnType><ReturnBitSize>8</ReturnBitSize><Parameter><Name>Key</Name><Type>STRING(160)</Type><BitSize>1288</BitSize></Parameter><Local><Name>page</Name><Type>INT</Type><BitSize>16</BitSize></Local><Local><Name>i</Name><Type>INT</Type><BitSize>16</BitSize></Local><Local><Name>pages</Name><Type>INT</Type><BitSize>16</BitSize></Local></Method><Method><Name>Registration_publishes_a_complete_capability</Name></Method><Method><Name>Range_type_and_domain_are_enforced</Name></Method><Method><Name>Stale_revision_is_refused</Name></Method><Method><Name>Write_reaches_the_owning_child_through_the_walk</Name></Method><Method><Name>Unknown_key_and_foreign_scope_change_nothing</Name></Method><Method><Name>RequiresReady_follows_the_root_state</Name></Method><Properties><Property><Name>PouType</Name><Value>FunctionBlock</Value></Property></Properties></DataType><DataType><Name Namespace="Fraktal_Core">FB_LocalConfigStore</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ Core §3.8b — the SHIPPED DEFAULT parameter-set store: named sets of
 (Scope, WriteKey) records held in the PLC's own retentive memory, so a
 conforming station persists its parametrization with no file system, no
 network and no configuration (E_ConfigStore.LOCAL_RETAIN).

 It stores RECORDS, not an image. That is not a stylistic choice: §3.8b says a
 byte image of a station's configuration shall not be used, because it
 reinterprets retained bytes silently when a type changes, carries no
 per-value identity for a file/database/MES to bind to, and cannot reject one
 bad value without rejecting all of them. Every value in here is individually
 addressable by (Scope, WriteKey) and is replayed through the owning module's
 typed handler on load, exactly as a single WRITE_CONFIG would be - so a set
 from a slightly different build fails on the one record that no longer fits
 instead of corrupting the machine wholesale.

 This store validates NOTHING and authorizes NOTHING (§5.6, §14). It moves
 records. The root owns CONFIG_SET, and the owning module owns the value.

 Memory: MAX_CONFIG_SETS × MAX_SET_RECORDS records of ~340 B live in the
 PERSISTENT area, so raising either constant costs the controller's persistent
 budget linearly. A deployment that needs more sets sizes the PLC persistent
 area to match, or substitutes a FILE_JSON/EXTERNAL store.]]></Comment><BitSize>447744</BitSize><Implements Namespace="Fraktal_Core">I_ConfigStore</Implements><SubItem><Name>_schema</Name><Type>UINT</Type><Comment><![CDATA[ §3.8b - versioned exactly like every other retained image. Version 0 means
 never written, so a first boot initializes silently; any other
 unrecognized version means a real image was rejected and the whole table
 is cleared rather than read by field position.]]></Comment><BitSize>16</BitSize><BitOffs>128</BitOffs><Properties><Property><Name>TcPersistent</Name></Property></Properties></SubItem><SubItem><Name>_info</Name><Type Namespace="Fraktal_Core">ST_ConfigSetInfo</Type><ArrayInfo><LBound>1</LBound><Elements>4</Elements></ArrayInfo><BitSize>8064</BitSize><BitOffs>160</BitOffs><Properties><Property><Name>TcPersistent</Name></Property></Properties></SubItem><SubItem><Name>_records</Name><Type Namespace="Fraktal_Core">ST_ConfigRecord</Type><ArrayInfo><LBound>1</LBound><Elements>4</Elements></ArrayInfo><ArrayInfo><LBound>1</LBound><Elements>32</Elements></ArrayInfo><BitSize>348160</BitSize><BitOffs>8224</BitOffs><Properties><Property><Name>TcPersistent</Name></Property></Properties></SubItem><SubItem><Name>_saveSlot</Name><Type>INT</Type><Comment><![CDATA[ 0 = no save open]]></Comment><BitSize>16</BitSize><BitOffs>356384</BitOffs></SubItem><SubItem><Name>_saveInfo</Name><Type Namespace="Fraktal_Core">ST_ConfigSetInfo</Type><BitSize>2016</BitSize><BitOffs>356416</BitOffs></SubItem><SubItem><Name>_saveCount</Name><Type>INT</Type><BitSize>16</BitSize><BitOffs>358432</BitOffs></SubItem><SubItem><Name>_saveFailed</Name><Type>BOOL</Type><BitSize>8</BitSize><BitOffs>358448</BitOffs></SubItem><SubItem><Name>_staged</Name><Type Namespace="Fraktal_Core">FB_ConfigSnapshot</Type><BitSize>89216</BitSize><BitOffs>358464</BitOffs></SubItem><SubItem><Name>_initialized</Name><Type>BOOL</Type><BitSize>8</BitSize><BitOffs>447680</BitOffs></SubItem><SubItem><Name>STORE_SCHEMA</Name><Type>UINT</Type><BitSize>16</BitSize><BitOffs>447696</BitOffs><Default><Value>1</Value></Default></SubItem><Method><Name>M_Kind</Name><ReturnType Namespace="Fraktal_Core">E_ConfigStore</ReturnType><ReturnBitSize>32</ReturnBitSize></Method><Method><Name>M_SaveBegin</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ Open a save. The target slot is chosen now - reusing the one that already
 holds this name, else a free one - but nothing in it is touched until commit.]]></Comment><ReturnType>BOOL</ReturnType><ReturnBitSize>8</ReturnBitSize><Parameter><Name>Info</Name><Type Namespace="Fraktal_Core">ST_ConfigSetInfo</Type><BitSize>2016</BitSize></Parameter><Local><Name>i</Name><Type>INT</Type><BitSize>16</BitSize></Local></Method><Method><Name>M_Delete</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ Free the slot. Clearing Used is the single assignment that makes the set
 unreachable, as setting it is what publishes one in M_SaveCommit; the
 records behind it are left to be overwritten by the next save into the slot.]]></Comment><ReturnType>BOOL</ReturnType><ReturnBitSize>8</ReturnBitSize><Parameter><Name>SetName</Name><Type>STRING(80)</Type><BitSize>648</BitSize></Parameter><Local><Name>slot</Name><Type>INT</Type><BitSize>16</BitSize></Local></Method><Method><Name>M_ReadInfo</Name><ReturnType>BOOL</ReturnType><ReturnBitSize>8</ReturnBitSize><Parameter><Name>SetName</Name><Type>STRING(80)</Type><BitSize>648</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>Info</Name><Type Namespace="Fraktal_Core" ReferenceTo="true">ST_ConfigSetInfo</Type><BitSize>64</BitSize><Properties><Property><Name>ItemType</Name><Value>InOut</Value></Property></Properties></Parameter><Local><Name>slot</Name><Type>INT</Type><BitSize>16</BitSize></Local></Method><Method><Name>_M_Find</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ Slot holding the named set, or 0. Exact match: a set name is an identity a
 client chose, not a prefix to guess at.]]></Comment><ReturnType>INT</ReturnType><ReturnBitSize>16</ReturnBitSize><Parameter><Name>SetName</Name><Type>STRING(80)</Type><BitSize>648</BitSize></Parameter><Local><Name>i</Name><Type>INT</Type><BitSize>16</BitSize></Local></Method><Method><Name>M_SaveCommit</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ Publish only after the complete volatile transaction succeeds.]]></Comment><ReturnType>BOOL</ReturnType><ReturnBitSize>8</ReturnBitSize><Local><Name>i</Name><Type>INT</Type><BitSize>16</BitSize></Local><Local><Name>record</Name><Type Namespace="Fraktal_Core">ST_ConfigRecord</Type><BitSize>2720</BitSize></Local></Method><Method><Name>M_ReadRecord</Name><ReturnType>BOOL</ReturnType><ReturnBitSize>8</ReturnBitSize><Parameter><Name>SetName</Name><Type>STRING(80)</Type><BitSize>648</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>Index</Name><Type>INT</Type><BitSize>16</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>Record</Name><Type Namespace="Fraktal_Core" ReferenceTo="true">ST_ConfigRecord</Type><BitSize>64</BitSize><Properties><Property><Name>ItemType</Name><Value>InOut</Value></Property></Properties></Parameter><Local><Name>slot</Name><Type>INT</Type><BitSize>16</BitSize></Local></Method><Method><Name>M_Count</Name><ReturnType>INT</ReturnType><ReturnBitSize>16</ReturnBitSize><Local><Name>i</Name><Type>INT</Type><BitSize>16</BitSize></Local></Method><Method><Name>_M_Init</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ §3.8b - one-shot retained-image check. Exactly the rule every StationCfg
 follows: version 0 initializes silently because nothing was lost; any other
 unrecognized version means a real image was rejected, so the table is cleared
 rather than interpreted by field position. A rejected SET table is reported
 through M_Count going to zero - the sets are simply not there, which is the
 honest answer and the one a client can act on.]]></Comment><Local><Name>i</Name><Type>INT</Type><BitSize>16</BitSize></Local></Method><Method><Name>M_SaveRecord</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ Stage one record without changing a published set.]]></Comment><ReturnType>BOOL</ReturnType><ReturnBitSize>8</ReturnBitSize><Parameter><Name>Record</Name><Type Namespace="Fraktal_Core">ST_ConfigRecord</Type><BitSize>2720</BitSize></Parameter></Method><Method><Name>M_InfoAt</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ Index runs over the OCCUPIED sets in slot order, so a client enumerating
 1..M_Count sees every set exactly once and no empty slots.]]></Comment><ReturnType>BOOL</ReturnType><ReturnBitSize>8</ReturnBitSize><Parameter><Name>Index</Name><Type>INT</Type><BitSize>16</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>Info</Name><Type Namespace="Fraktal_Core" ReferenceTo="true">ST_ConfigSetInfo</Type><BitSize>64</BitSize><Properties><Property><Name>ItemType</Name><Value>InOut</Value></Property></Properties></Parameter><Local><Name>i</Name><Type>INT</Type><BitSize>16</BitSize></Local><Local><Name>seen</Name><Type>INT</Type><BitSize>16</BitSize></Local></Method><Method><Name>M_SaveAbort</Name><ReturnType>BOOL</ReturnType><ReturnBitSize>8</ReturnBitSize></Method><Properties><Property><Name>PouType</Name><Value>FunctionBlock</Value></Property></Properties></DataType><DataType><Name>FB_ProbeConfigPersistence</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ §3.8b test double for I_ConfigPersistence. A real durability provider talks
 to the runtime over ADS and completes when it completes, which is not
 something a scan-stepped test can drive; this one is driven explicitly so a
 suite can hold a write PENDING, complete it, or fail it on demand.

 It is a TEST fixture and lives in the test project, not in the library: C8
 forbids exactly this shape shipping inside a reusable type (§5.7).]]></Comment><BitSize>192</BitSize><Implements Namespace="Fraktal_Core">I_ConfigPersistence</Implements><SubItem><Name>Requests</Name><Type>UDINT</Type><Comment><![CDATA[ proves the root actually asked, not just published]]></Comment><BitSize>32</BitSize><BitOffs>128</BitOffs><Properties><Property><Name>ItemType</Name><Value>Output</Value></Property></Properties></SubItem><SubItem><Name>_busy</Name><Type>BOOL</Type><BitSize>8</BitSize><BitOffs>160</BitOffs></SubItem><SubItem><Name>_failed</Name><Type>BOOL</Type><BitSize>8</BitSize><BitOffs>168</BitOffs></SubItem><SubItem><Name>_refuse</Name><Type>BOOL</Type><Comment><![CDATA[ M_Request itself is refused (a store that will not start)]]></Comment><BitSize>8</BitSize><BitOffs>176</BitOffs></SubItem><SubItem><Name>_holdBusy</Name><Type>BOOL</Type><Comment><![CDATA[ stay busy until the test says otherwise]]></Comment><BitSize>8</BitSize><BitOffs>184</BitOffs></SubItem><Method><Name>M_SetHold</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ Keep an accepted write in flight, so a suite can observe PersistPending.]]></Comment><Parameter><Name>Hold</Name><Type>BOOL</Type><BitSize>8</BitSize></Parameter></Method><Method><Name>M_Request</Name><ReturnType>BOOL</ReturnType><ReturnBitSize>8</ReturnBitSize></Method><Method><Name>M_Cyclic</Name><ReturnType>BOOL</ReturnType><ReturnBitSize>8</ReturnBitSize></Method><Method><Name>M_Failed</Name><ReturnType>BOOL</ReturnType><ReturnBitSize>8</ReturnBitSize></Method><Method><Name>M_SetOutcome</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ Decide what the NEXT completion reports. Refuse = the store will not even
 start, which §3.8b treats as a failure and not as a quiet no-op.]]></Comment><Parameter><Name>Failed</Name><Type>BOOL</Type><BitSize>8</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>Refuse</Name><Type>BOOL</Type><BitSize>8</BitSize></Parameter></Method><Properties><Property><Name>PouType</Name><Value>FunctionBlock</Value></Property></Properties></DataType><DataType><Name>FB_ConfigDurability_Tests</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ Core §3.8b under test, the half that is about TIME rather than content:

   * a retained image is restored, or the module runs on declared defaults -
     and the difference between "never written" and "written by a build that
     laid it out differently" is the difference between silence and an alarm;
   * an accepted write is LIVE, and stays PUBLISHED AS NOT DURABLE until the
     store says otherwise, within a window the deployment declares;
   * what a lost image COSTS is the deployment's declaration, not the module's:
     the same loss either annunciates and runs, or refuses START_STOP until an
     ENGINEER acknowledges it.

 The fixture is the same FB_ProbeConfigUnit/FB_ProbeConfigCM pair the §3.10.2
 write suite uses, on purpose: neither probe contains one line of persistence
 code, so if these pass, a station gets all of it for free (§1.1 O1).]]></Comment><BitSize>37221760</BitSize><ExtendsType Namespace="TcUnit">FB_TestSuite</ExtendsType><SubItem><Name>_u</Name><Type>FB_ProbeConfigUnit</Type><BitSize>3044928</BitSize><BitOffs>33574912</BitOffs></SubItem><SubItem><Name>_init</Name><Type>BOOL</Type><BitSize>8</BitSize><BitOffs>36619840</BitOffs></SubItem><SubItem><Name>_store</Name><Type Namespace="Fraktal_Core">FB_LocalConfigStore</Type><BitSize>447744</BitSize><BitOffs>36619904</BitOffs></SubItem><SubItem><Name>_persist</Name><Type>FB_ProbeConfigPersistence</Type><BitSize>192</BitSize><BitOffs>37067648</BitOffs></SubItem><SubItem><Name>_users</Name><Type Namespace="Fraktal_Core">FB_LocalAccessProvider</Type><BitSize>52480</BitSize><BitOffs>37067840</BitOffs></SubItem><SubItem><Name>_report</Name><Type Namespace="Fraktal_Core">ST_ReleaseReport</Type><BitSize>100640</BitSize><BitOffs>37120320</BitOffs></SubItem><SubItem><Name>_version</Name><Type>UINT</Type><Comment><![CDATA[ a stand-in retained StationCfg image]]></Comment><BitSize>16</BitSize><BitOffs>37220960</BitOffs></SubItem><SubItem><Name>_path</Name><Type>STRING(80)</Type><BitSize>648</BitSize><BitOffs>37220976</BitOffs></SubItem><SubItem><Name>_expected</Name><Type>UINT</Type><BitSize>16</BitSize><BitOffs>37221632</BitOffs></SubItem><SubItem><Name>_found</Name><Type>UINT</Type><BitSize>16</BitSize><BitOffs>37221648</BitOffs></SubItem><SubItem><Name>_taken</Name><Type>BOOL</Type><BitSize>8</BitSize><BitOffs>37221664</BitOffs></SubItem><SubItem><Name>_released</Name><Type>BOOL</Type><BitSize>8</BitSize><BitOffs>37221672</BitOffs></SubItem><SubItem><Name>_i</Name><Type>INT</Type><BitSize>16</BitSize><BitOffs>37221680</BitOffs></SubItem><SubItem><Name>_firstPass</Name><Type>BOOL</Type><Comment><![CDATA[ The window test is the one test here that must span scans (TIME() does not
 advance inside one), so the other seven run exactly once: re-run each scan,
 their writes would re-arm the very window being measured.]]></Comment><BitSize>8</BitSize><BitOffs>37221696</BitOffs></SubItem><SubItem><Name>_winArmed</Name><Type>BOOL</Type><BitSize>8</BitSize><BitOffs>37221704</BitOffs></SubItem><SubItem><Name>_winScans</Name><Type>INT</Type><BitSize>16</BitSize><BitOffs>37221712</BitOffs></SubItem><Method><Name>The_root_annunciates_a_loss_without_blocking_on_its_own</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ §3.8b: the annunciation is AUTO_RESET and therefore never blocking. The
 consequence belongs to E_ConfigRestorePolicy, so a station that declared
 DEFAULTS_AND_ANNUNCIATE really does keep running while the alarm stands.]]></Comment></Method><Method><Name>An_accepted_write_is_pending_until_the_store_completes</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ §3.8b: "Transport acknowledgement and Accepted mean a value is LIVE, not that
 it is DURABLE." This is that sentence as a test.]]></Comment></Method><Method><Name>An_elapsed_window_publishes_PersistFailed</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ §3.8b declares a BOUNDED window, and a bound nobody measures is not a bound.
 WindowMs := 0 declares "durable in the same scan or not at all", which is the
 honest declaration for a store that cannot be slow.]]></Comment></Method><Method><Name>First_boot_initializes_silently</Name></Method><Method><Name>_M_Arm</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ One-time wiring, on the first test rather than in a constructor. The store and
 the durability stub are INJECTED the way a composition root injects them, so
 these tests drive the same surface MAIN does.]]></Comment></Method><Method><Name>A_store_that_refuses_publishes_PersistFailed</Name></Method><Method><Name>Blocking_policy_refuses_start_until_an_engineer_acknowledges</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ §3.8b BLOCK_UNTIL_ACKNOWLEDGED: no START_STOP release until an operator of at
 least ENGINEER level accepts the loss. The release is restored by the
 ACKNOWLEDGEMENT, never by the loss going away - nothing brings a lost image back.]]></Comment></Method><Method><Name>_M_TakeLoss</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ Take every outstanding restore loss from the root, exactly as OnCyclic does,
 and leave the LAST one in the suite's fields.]]></Comment><ReturnType>BOOL</ReturnType><ReturnBitSize>8</ReturnBitSize><Local><Name>guard</Name><Type>INT</Type><BitSize>16</BitSize></Local><Local><Name>path</Name><Type>STRING(80)</Type><BitSize>648</BitSize></Local><Local><Name>expected</Name><Type>UINT</Type><BitSize>16</BitSize></Local><Local><Name>found</Name><Type>UINT</Type><BitSize>16</BitSize></Local></Method><Method><Name>An_unrecognized_version_is_reported_once_with_its_owner</Name></Method><Method><Name>A_matching_version_restores_and_reports_nothing</Name></Method><Properties><Property><Name>PouType</Name><Value>FunctionBlock</Value></Property></Properties></DataType><DataType><Name>FB_ConfigSet_Tests</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ Core §3.8b under test, the half that is about CONTENT:

   * a set is a PROJECTION of the same deterministic walk that builds the
     §3.10.2 manifest - not a second list of what a station's configuration
     consists of, which would be free to drift from the first;
   * it is records keyed by (Scope, WriteKey), never a byte image, which is
     what lets one bad value be rejected without rejecting all of them;
   * a load is STAGED and ALL-OR-NOTHING, so a set that is wrong anywhere
     changes nothing anywhere;
   * and the whole set moves as TEXT, because a set that cannot leave the
     controller is just retentive memory with a name on it.]]></Comment><BitSize>40322432</BitSize><ExtendsType Namespace="TcUnit">FB_TestSuite</ExtendsType><SubItem><Name>_u</Name><Type>FB_ProbeConfigUnit</Type><BitSize>3044928</BitSize><BitOffs>33574912</BitOffs></SubItem><SubItem><Name>_init</Name><Type>BOOL</Type><BitSize>8</BitSize><BitOffs>36619840</BitOffs></SubItem><SubItem><Name>_store</Name><Type Namespace="Fraktal_Core">FB_LocalConfigStore</Type><Comment><![CDATA[ PL_Fraktal.MAX_CONFIG_SETS is 4 and the store never evicts a set, so every
 DELIBERATELY WRONG import shares the one name 'imported' (each overwrites
 the last). Seven distinct names filled the store by the fifth test, and the
