/BitSize><BitOffs>34971520</BitOffs><Default><DateTime>T#60S</DateTime></Default></SubItem><SubItem><Name>GENERIC_FOLDER</Name><Type>STRING(80)</Type><BitSize>648</BitSize><BitOffs>34971552</BitOffs><Default><String>C:\ProgramData\Fraktal\Tests\Medium\</String></Default></SubItem><Method><Name>A_file_medium_creates_its_folder</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ The station's data folder is an absolute path the medium creates on its first
 request (the press uses C:\ProgramData\Fraktal\PressDemo\).]]></Comment></Method><Method><Name>_M_Key</Name><ReturnType>STRING(80)</ReturnType><ReturnBitSize>648</ReturnBitSize><Parameter><Name>Name</Name><Type>STRING(40)</Type><BitSize>328</BitSize></Parameter></Method><Method><Name>A_file_document_survives_a_torn_write_and_reports_a_loss</Name><Local><Name>key</Name><Type>STRING(80)</Type><BitSize>648</BitSize></Local></Method><Method><Name>Requests_are_refused_or_answered_never_left_waiting</Name><Local><Name>answer</Name><Type Namespace="Fraktal_Core">ST_PersistResult</Type><BitSize>96</BitSize></Local><Local><Name>queued</Name><Type>INT</Type><BitSize>16</BitSize></Local><Local><Name>i</Name><Type>INT</Type><BitSize>16</BitSize></Local></Method><Method><Name>The_newest_copy_wins_in_either_slot</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ Regression (live acceptance 2026-10-06): with the newest copy in slot a and an
 older one in slot b, a fresh run returned nothing - the payload check compared
 slot a against slot b's header - and its next write then replaced slot a, the
 newest copy. A fresh medium must read slot a, and must write the next copy to b.]]></Comment><Local><Name>key</Name><Type>STRING(80)</Type><BitSize>648</BitSize></Local></Method><Method><Name>The_retentive_pool_keeps_documents_and_says_when_it_is_full</Name></Method><Properties><Property><Name>PouType</Name><Value>FunctionBlock</Value></Property></Properties></DataType><DataType><Name Namespace="Fraktal_Core">FB_MediumConfigStore</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ Core §3.8b - named parameter sets kept on any I_PersistMedium: each set is
 one FB_ConfigSetDocument ('<KeyPrefix>.set<n>'), so on the file medium a set
 is a readable JSON-lines file and on the retentive medium a pooled document.
 The project picks the medium; this store is the same either way.

 The sets are held in volatile slots for the synchronous I_ConfigStore reads,
 read back from the medium once at start, and every saved or deleted set is
 written out. It is also this store's I_ConfigPersistence: the owning root finds
 it by __QUERYINTERFACE and drives it with every other durability provider, so
 PersistPending covers a saved set until its document is written (§3.8b), and
 a project wires nothing beyond SetConfigStore.

 Like every store it validates nothing and authorizes nothing (§5.6, §14); a
 set restored here is still validated by the root when somebody loads it. A set
 lost on the medium is reported by RestoreLost and is simply not listed, as
